# Container Environment

You are running inside an isolated Docker container. Whether your workspace under `/workspace` reaches the host depends on the launch mode: in **dir-mounted** mode (the default) it is a host bind mount, so edits land on the user's disk; in **self-hosted** mode (`--isolated`) it is a container-local Docker volume holding a clone, so nothing written there is visible to the user and work normally leaves only by `git push` / a PR.
Check the mode at runtime with the `POWBOX_WORKSPACE_HOST_PATH` env var — set and non-empty means a host bind mount (its value is the workspace's host path), unset means container-local. Use it before you promise the user a local file — and note that `.worktrees/` is on container-local storage (`wt-bootstrap` fails closed otherwise), so a file inside a task worktree is not on the user's disk either. `/ctx/<name>` folders are host bind mounts too (read-only unless launched `:rw`). You have full autonomy (`${AGENT_AUTONOMY_FLAG}`) — the container and its network firewall are your safety boundary.

Your identity for this session is in `$CONTAINER_NAME` (also available via the `cid` command).

