## Delegating to another agent

This container ships more than one agent harness. Besides yourself, the following peer agent(s) are installed, on `PATH`, and already configured (each keeps its own persistent login and skills in this container):

${AGENT_PEERS}

Invoke a peer directly when it helps — e.g. ask the other agent for an independent review of your work, or hand a self-contained sub-task to it. A peer shares this container and its `/workspace` tree (bind mount or self-hosted volume alike), so it sees the same files you do. Run it non-interactively for a one-shot task (the one-shot forms above; a single invocation runs a full agentic loop, so one prompt is enough for a thorough review with a verdict and findings), or interactively to hand off the session.

Peer opinions are **best-effort**: if the binary is missing, the login preflight fails, or it reports exhausted usage, treat that as "no second opinion available" and continue on your own judgment — don't block or error out. Retry only clearly transient failures, once or twice. When delegating a review, give the peer the concrete scope (base branch or commit range, worktree path) and ask for a verdict plus numbered findings so the result is directly actionable.

### Scratchpad discipline

The session scratchpad is **one directory shared by every concurrently running subagent** — "session-specific" means the session, not the subagent.
So every file parallel work writes there MUST carry a per-task unique name (suffix the worktree slug or the PR number) or live in a `mktemp -d -p <scratchpad>` subdirectory — a `$$` PID suffix does not survive to the next tool call, which gets a fresh shell.
Fixed names have already crossed streams here: two reviewers both writing `verify.log`, one then validating the other's worktree.
When you fan work out, state this rule in each subagent's prompt — that is the only way it learns it has siblings.

## Git and GitHub

- This container authenticates to GitHub via **HTTPS** using the `gh` credential helper, which is configured automatically at startup. There are no SSH keys in the container.
- Prefer `gh` for remote operations: `gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`, etc. These use the GitHub API directly and are not affected by the remote URL protocol.
- Host-mounted workspaces often have an SSH-form `origin` remote (`git@github.com:...`). You do **not** need to rewrite it: a container-local `insteadOf` rewrite transparently routes SSH-form GitHub remotes through HTTPS + the gh credential helper, so `git push origin`, `git fetch`, and `gh pr create` just work with no action needed. The rewrite lives only in the container's git config — the host repo's remote URL is left untouched.

- `git` is available for all local operations (status, diff, log, commit, branch, etc.).
- `gitcat [--raw] <ref> <path> [<start> [<end>]]` prints a file as of another branch or commit with stable line numbers (a range slice keeps full-file numbering; `--raw` emits plain bytes for piping). Use it for cross-branch reads — e.g. inspecting code across a stacked-PR chain from one checkout — instead of `git show <ref>:<path> > /tmp/...` round-trips or switching branches.
- `gh-review-threads [--all] [--repo <owner>/<repo>] <PR#>` fetches a PR's inline review threads as a JSON array on stdout — each thread with `id`, `isResolved`, `isOutdated`, `path`, `line`, and `comments[]` (`databaseId`, `author { login __typename }`, `body`, `diffHunk`, `url`); unresolved-only by default, `--all` includes resolved. It exists because, run concurrently with other `gh` GraphQL calls (e.g. an `address-reviews` fan-out), `gh api graphql --paginate` has returned **another PR's** threads; the helper pages manually (fresh per-page call, never `--paginate`) and asserts every comment `url` belongs to the requested PR, retrying once then failing closed (exit `3`, nothing on stdout) on a contaminated response. Use it for review-addressing instead of a hand-rolled `reviewThreads` query.
- `dc-enter [--replace] <slug> [<ref>]` / `dc-remove <slug>` give you a **disposable clone** of the repository you are standing in — a place to verify a claim empirically that is safe to wreck. Use them whenever a check wants to delete refs, rewrite history, `gc`, or run a destructive command; never improvise a clone-and-`cd`, and never do it in the checkout itself. A worktree is **not** a substitute: it isolates the working tree but shares `.git`, so `branch -f`, `reset`, `update-ref`, `gc` and `push` all reach every sibling worktree. Only a real clone isolates refs and objects.
  - Calling convention: stdout carries **only** the clone's absolute path, every diagnostic goes to stderr — so `DC="$(dc-enter probe)"` yields either a usable path or an empty string plus a non-zero status. Check it before using it (`[ -n "$DC" ] && [ -d "$DC/.git" ] || exit 1`); an unguarded empty `$DC` in `rm -rf "$DC"/*` is the incident this pair exists to prevent. The default clone is at the **invoking worktree's** HEAD (a nested subdirectory is fine); an optional `<ref>` picks another branch/tag/commit.
  - **Clear `GIT_DIR` and its relatives in your own shell** before using `$DC`: they override `git -C`, so `git -C "$DC" update-ref -d …` would hit the invoking repository however the path reads. `dc-enter` drops them from its own process and warns on stderr naming what it dropped, but `unset` cannot reach your shell. `unset GIT_DIR GIT_WORK_TREE GIT_INDEX_FILE …` (`git rev-parse --local-env-vars` prints the whole set), or run the consumer under `env -u GIT_DIR …`.
  - A slug that already has a clone is **refused, not reused** — a concurrent sibling may be verifying in it. `dc-remove <slug>` frees it (a no-op when there is none, so a cleanup trap can call it unconditionally), or `dc-enter --replace <slug>` re-derives it pristine. `dc-remove` takes a slug only, never a path, and refuses any directory `dc-enter` did not create and mark — so free the slug with it rather than reaching for `rm -rf` yourself.
- The global git config is re-copied from the host mount at **every** container start (the container's own settings are re-applied on top), so hand edits to it do not survive a restart. Do not modify `/home/node/.gitconfig-host` (read-only mount).
- Never rewrite published history with raw `git push --force`/`-f` — a concurrent push to the old tip would be silently clobbered. Use `git push --force-with-lease` (ideally the exact `--force-with-lease=<branch>:<sha>`). The container sets `push.useForceIfIncludes=true`, so a bare lease also rejects a rewrite that never integrated the current remote tip (override a deliberate one with `--no-force-if-includes`); this hardens `--force-with-lease` only and does nothing for raw `--force`. Keeping one writer per PR branch is the strongest safeguard.

### Worktree helpers

Parallel, worktree-isolated work uses three baked helpers on `PATH`. Skills and workflows that create worktrees here should prefer these over raw `git worktree` commands — they encode powbox-specific placement and safety that vanilla git does not.

- `wt-bootstrap` — one-shot session bootstrap: verifies the worktree roots are container-local (never the host bind mount), reaps only this container's orphaned worktrees (safely — see below), probes remote push access, and reports storage headroom as a single JSON object. Exit non-zero means STOP. Worktrees are placed under `.worktrees/$CONTAINER_NAME/` — **per-container** storage (keyed by agent + project). Commit and push rather than trusting a worktree to survive a container recycle: it survives one only when `.worktrees` is the persistent volume, which also backs `.git/worktrees` (`.worktrees/.gitworktrees`), and the tmpfs fallback loses both. `.claude/worktrees` (harness-native working trees) is an ephemeral tmpfs shadow. Orphan reaping never deletes work: a dir that is no longer a live worktree is removed only when empty; if it has content (e.g. a pre-durable-metadata leftover) it is preserved under `.worktrees/.orphaned/` and reported as `quarantinedOrphans`. If `wt-bootstrap` blocks because a root is not a mountpoint, the fix in dir-mounted mode is the `enable-worktrees` skill (it declares the shadows in `.powbox.yml`/`.gitignore`); rebuild/relaunch if the image predates durable worktree metadata.
- `wt-enter <slug> <branch> [<base>]` — rerun-safely resolve (reuse / attach / create) a task worktree under `.worktrees/$CONTAINER_NAME/<slug>` and print ONLY its path on stdout, so callers can `WT="$(wt-enter …)"`. Omit `<base>` for stages that must never create work (e.g. a reviewer attaching to an implementer's worktree — a missing branch is then an error, not a silent empty checkout).
- `wt-remove <slug> [--force]` — remove a task worktree, but NEVER work: it **refuses even with `--force`** when git has left **any operation it itself tracks** in progress there — a rebase (either backend), a `git am`, a merge, a cherry-pick, a revert, a multi-commit cherry-pick/revert sequence, a **bisect**, or a conflicted **`git notes merge`** — when the tree has uncommitted changes, or when the worktree's own git metadata cannot be located or read, or its status cannot be read at all, so its state cannot be established (unknown ⇒ refuse; a `git status` that FAILS is not a clean tree just because it printed nothing). That is the opposite of vanilla `git worktree remove --force`, which discards all of it. Several of those states leave `git status` **clean** — a notes merge leaves it saying nothing at all, since its whole conflict lives in the worktree's admin dir — so they are detected from the same per-worktree state git itself consults, not from the porcelain — both places git keeps it: the on-disk state files **and** the ref backend, since `CHERRY_PICK_HEAD`, `REVERT_HEAD`, `NOTES_MERGE_PARTIAL` and `NOTES_MERGE_REF` are pseudo-refs that leave no file at all under the `reftable` ref storage (which is asked twice: `NOTES_MERGE_REF` is a *symbolic* ref, so "does this name resolve" and "does this name exist" are different questions, and only the second one still answers yes once the notes ref it points at is deleted). The operation is checked before the uncommitted-changes case, so a worktree stopped on a conflict is told which operation it is in rather than merely that it is dirty. Both the state and the status are read through the worktree's **own** git dir (resolved from the repository's registrations), never as `git -C <wt>`, which resolves upward into the enclosing checkout for a worktree that has lost its `.git` pointer. It also refuses to remove the worktree you are standing in. A refusal names the operation **and the state marker it was found by**, and points at inspection (`git -C <path> status`, plus `git -C <path> bisect log` for a bisect) — never at a way to discard it; if you truly want a mid-operation worktree gone, vanilla `git worktree remove --force` is the honest way to ask. The branch is always kept — commits/refs persist in the shared `.git`. `--force` is passed through only after the clean checks pass (e.g. for ignored build artifacts git won't auto-remove).

  "What git tracks" is the exact limit of that promise, and exactly one shape falls outside it: an unconcluded `git merge --squash`, which git does not record as an operation at all (no `MERGE_HEAD`, and `git merge --abort` answers "There is no merge to abort"). Guarding its `SQUASH_MSG` marker was measured and rejected, because the marker **outlives** the operation — `git restore --staged --worktree .` abandons a squash without clearing it, leaving `SQUASH_MSG` beside a fully clean tree that git considers idle — so a guard would refuse worktrees holding nothing in flight. In practice a squash's staged result trips the uncommitted-changes check; only a squash that produced **no net change** is removed, and all that is lost is its pending commit message. Both directions are pinned in `scripts/test-wt-orphan-safety.sh`.

**Shared vs. isolated surfaces (read before any broad cleanup).** These helpers scope worktree *creation, pruning, and removal* by `$CONTAINER_NAME`, but the container's `.worktrees` storage **and the repository's main checkout are shared by every process in this container** — including a peer harness you invoke (see "Delegating to another agent" above); the per-`$CONTAINER_NAME` subdir isolates the worktrees, not the main working tree. Two separate agent containers instead get separate `.worktrees` storage, and `--isolated` gives each a full private clone. So when working in parallel, keep every edit and cleanup inside your assigned worktree (`git -C <your-worktree> …`) and commit checkpoints often. Ordinary Git recovery is not forbidden, but a broad `git reset --hard` — and especially `git clean -fdx`, which ignores `.gitignore` and so also wipes the `.worktrees` scaffolding — run against the shared main checkout can discard a concurrent peer's uncommitted files; if you genuinely need one, pause or finish sibling work first.

## Filesystem layout

| Path | What it is |
|------|------------|
| `/workspace/<project-slug>` | Project source, read-write; the working directory (launch modes and host visibility: see the top of this file). Slug is `<name>-<hash>` dir-mounted, `<repo-slug>[-<name-slug>]-<instance-hash>` under `--isolated` (the `agent-ws-*` volume) |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/node_modules` | Per-container Docker volume (Linux packages, separate from host); dir-mounted JS/powbox projects only |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/<subpackage>/node_modules` | Ephemeral tmpfs shadow (auto-detected for monorepo workspaces; the `pnpm`/`pn` wrapper re-shadows before installs, so a package added mid-session is covered automatically — in dir-mounted mode, run `shadow-refresh.sh` by hand only if you populate `node_modules` another way) |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/<dotnet-project>/{bin,obj}` | Ephemeral tmpfs shadow (auto-detected beside every `*.csproj`/`*.fsproj`/`*.vbproj`, matched case-insensitively so a Windows-authored `Legacy.CSPROJ` counts too, ensuring a container `dotnet build` never writes container-absolute paths into `obj/` on the host bind mount where a host build would trip over them). Build output does not survive container recreation; in dir-mounted mode, run `shadow-refresh.sh` after adding a project mid-session. An existing `bin`/`obj` that is a symlink, or that holds Git-tracked files (a project redirecting output via `ArtifactsPath` may keep real content there), is left un-shadowed so nothing real is masked |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/.worktrees` | Per-container Docker volume for git worktrees (dir-mounted JS/powbox, `go.mod`, or boundedly detected .NET projects); also holds durable per-worktree git metadata at `.worktrees/.gitworktrees` (bind-mounted over `.git/worktrees`). It also reserves cache locations for tools the tooling table above does not list, which stay unused unless a layer set (see the end of this file) or your own install adds the tool: Go module/build caches (`.gomodcache`, `.gocache`, and per-worktree `.golangci-cache/…`), the NuGet packages folder `.worktrees/.nuget` (`NUGET_PACKAGES`), the ccache directory `.worktrees/.ccache` (`CCACHE_DIR`), and the shared Playwright browsers cache `.worktrees/.ms-playwright` (`PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`) |
| `/ctx` | Optional context mount root; external folders are mounted under `/ctx/<name>` via `--ctx` or `ctx:` config |
| `${AGENT_CONFIG_DIR}` | ${AGENT_NAME} config/state (persistent Docker volume) |
| `/home/node/.config/gh` | GitHub CLI config (persistent Docker volume) |
| `/workspace/<project-slug>/.worktrees/.pnpm-store` | Per-container pnpm store, dir-mounted JS/powbox gate only (inside the `.worktrees` volume; co-located so worktree installs hardlink) |

Under `--isolated` the `node_modules`, `.worktrees`, `.git/worktrees`, and store/cache paths above (and the worktree roots `wt-bootstrap` checks) are plain subdirectories of the single `agent-ws-*` volume, not separate volumes, and the tmpfs shadows are skipped entirely — running `shadow-refresh.sh` there is a harmless no-op, because the script skips itself under `POWBOX_SELF_HOSTED=1` and exits 0 without mounting.

## Network

Private/local networks (10.x, 172.16.x, 192.168.x, link-local) are blocked by the container firewall. Public internet is fully open for npm, GitHub, web research, and API calls.

## Installing additional packages

- **System packages:** `sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y <package>` — sudo is allowed for `apt-get`.
- **Python packages:** `pip3 install <package>` — no `--break-system-packages` flag needed.
